# wiredown

wiredown describes UI screens as text in a markdown fence tagged `wiredown` (`wiremark` and `screens` also draw). The text is drawn as static wireframes: describe structure, not pixels or colours.

- Before writing a block, get the language: the `get_wiredown_grammar` MCP tool, or `npx wiredown contract`. Use only the kinds, attributes and flags it lists.
- One fenced block is one flow. Start with optional headers (`@device phone`, `@title "Text"`, `@flow a b c`), then `screen <id> "Label"` lines with their elements indented below.
- Indent children by 2 or 4 spaces, one step per level, spaces only. Labels are in double quotes.
- `-> id` must name a screen in the same block (or `back`).
- Give an explicit `#id` to any element you link to, patch or refer to later.
- Show states as `variant <id> of <screen> "Label"` with indented patch lines such as `#password error="Wrong password"`.
- After writing or changing a block, check it with the `lint_wiredown` MCP tool or `npx wiredown lint <file>`, and fix every error.
- `wiredown_link` returns a playground link that draws the block; `npx wiredown link <file>` prints the same link.
